---
description: Enforces Next.js 14/15 App Router patterns with React Server Components. Activate when writing Next.js or React code in an App Router project.
globs: ["**/*.tsx", "**/*.ts", "**/next.config.*", "app/**/*"]
alwaysApply: false
---
<🎯 Title>
Next.js App Router & React Server Components Instructions

## Rules
- Default ALL components to React Server Components — add `'use client'` ONLY at interactive leaf nodes (event listeners, hooks like useState/useEffect).
- NEVER put `'use client'` at the top of layout.tsx or page.tsx unless absolutely necessary.
- ALWAYS protect Server Actions with Zod schema validation + authentication checks before DB operations.
- ALWAYS use `route.ts` handlers with standard Web Request/Response APIs (not legacy `req.query`).
- ALWAYS use Metadata API (`generateMetadata`, `metadata` export) for SEO — NEVER use `<Head>` component.
- ALWAYS use strict caching: use `revalidateTag()` / `revalidatePath()` for on-demand revalidation.
- ALWAYS optimize images: use `next/image` with explicit width/height or `fill` prop.
- PREFER parallel routes (`@slot`) and intercepting routes (`(.)`, `(..)`) for modal patterns.
- ALWAYS use loading UI: `loading.tsx` for Suspense boundaries, `error.tsx` for error boundaries.
- NEVER use `useRouter()` from `next/router` — use `next/navigation` in App Router.
- NEVER use `getServerSideProps` or `getStaticProps` — these are Pages Router patterns.
- PREFER `fetch()` with `next: { revalidate: N }` or `cache: 'no-store'` for data fetching.
- ALWAYS collocate components: keep related components in the same route segment folder.

## Verification
- Run `npm run build` to catch RSC violations.
- Run `npm run lint` to ensure standard compliance.
